		{
			"id": "memo_group",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**Memos** (8) — Ctrl+hover a link to preview\n\n- [[S2M5]] — tasks need a concrete communicative purpose\n- [[S4M4]] — domain interest and audience make models matter\n- [[S5M1]] — team coordination carries the modelling work\n- [[S3M6]] — layout choices when others will analyse the model\n- [[L2M5]] — domain and course rhythm gate modeling uptake\n- [[L4M3]] — domain freedom trades lecturer workload for fairness\n- [[S7M5]] — new syntax at the switch; a real reader later\n- [[S10M5]] — exclusive notation ownership split the system",
			"x": -40,
			"y": 80,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 304,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "code_group",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**Codes** (26) — Ctrl+hover a link to preview\n\n- [[L2-C17]] — domain understanding is the hardest learning condition\n- [[L2-C18]] — one shared project domain chosen for fairness\n- [[L2-C19]] — attendance and workload pull students away from class\n- [[L2-C22]] — modeling confined to one unit; low student interest\n- [[L4-C06]] — student project freedom increases lecturer workload\n- [[L4-C07]] — domain choice driven by student fairness\n- [[L4-C08]] — modelling needs problems with real complexity\n- [[L4-C11]] — lecturer sees students want clearer tasks under pressure\n- [[S2-C10]] — modelling was one chore among many\n- [[S2-C28]] — modelling taught in passing needs emphasis\n- [[S2-C29]] — generic modelling tasks lack clear purpose\n- [[S3-C19]] — motivation affects speed more than quality\n- [[S3-C20]] — cared about readability for thesis analysis\n- [[S4-C11]] — modelling works when one person owns the design\n- [[S4-C14]] — interesting domain motivates careful modelling\n- [[S4-C15]] — real reader audience makes diagrams communicative\n- [[S5-C03]] — group reads brief aloud then assigns drafter\n- [[S5-C06]] — good enough means teammates understand scope\n- [[S5-C09]] — shared Miro model with weekly diagram stand-ups\n- [[S5-C13]] — external readers push stakeholder vocabulary\n- [[S7-C01]] — same idea taught with different course notations\n- [[S7-C14]] — switching notations confuses syntax not starting\n- [[S7-C15]] — thesis reader made modelling a priority\n- [[S10-C01]] — thesis diagrams for communication not strict UML\n- [[S10-C12]] — split-by-notation made two versions of the system\n- [[S10-C13]] — explainability became a quality criterion",
			"x": 580,
			"y": 80,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 808,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "hub",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "CAT10 — Real audience makes modelling worth it\nRQ3 · finding",
			"x": 300,
			"y": -180,
			"width": 400,
			"height": 100,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "explainer",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "What this means\n\nModelling feels worth the effort when a real person will read it (supervisor, client, teammate). An “interesting topic” alone is a weaker motivator. The same student may treat coursework models as low-stakes and thesis diagrams as a priority.",
